{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1372162-question-about-book-compatibility","id":1372162,"title":"Question about book compatibility","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-03-01T09:42:47-08:00","id":1372162,"title":"Question about book compatibility","content":"I hope this isn\u2019t a dumb question, but which if any books from New World of Darkness are compatible with Chronicles of Darkness? So, if my intent is to play Chronicles of Darkness, would I be able to use the Armory book from NWOD? What about the innocents or Asylum books? Also I am wondering the same thing about the splats. Are the source books for Vampire the requiem 1e compatible with Vampire the requiem 2e? There are so many interesting books already made for NWOD, but I don\u2019t want to buy a bunch of stuff that isn\u2019t compatible. I often see people say in the forums that chronicles of darkness is the same as new world of darkness, but that isn\u2019t true is it? Aren\u2019t the rules changed?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26006-jcsingleton1834","name":"Jcsingleton1834","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-01T10:32:38-08:00","id":1372170,"title":"","content":"NWoD was rebranded as Chronicles if I recall how it went correctly. So your books should be fine when dealing Chronicles to New World.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3400-nalak42","name":"nalak42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3400_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2931"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-01T10:57:16-08:00","id":1372171,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Jcsingleton1834</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/1372162-question-about-book-compatibility#post1372162\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I often see people say in the forums that chronicles of darkness is the same as new world of darkness, but that isn\u2019t true is it? Aren\u2019t the rules changed?</div> </div> </div> </div>Chronicles of Darkness is just a rebranding. It's the same game as (new) World of Darkness. The rebranding happened around the time nWoD/CofD got its second edition out.<br /> <br /> Because there's an edition change, not everything is fully compatible, but basically no setting material has changed between editions. Ruleswise, it's fairly easy to just convert most stuff, but I would recommend starting with second edition material first and get a feel for the power levels and stuff like that. The only first edition supplements I would discourage you from getting would be the two Armory books. They contain a lot of mechanics and lists that aren't balanced to second edition (though some of the things were never particularly balanced), and the second edition supplement Hurt Locker fulfils mostly the same niche so you're better off getting that one instead.<br /> <br /> The other games, like Vampire: The Requiem, Changeling: The Lost, Mummy: The Curse, etc, have varying degrees of changes in their special rules. Vampire is the one that has changed the least, so all those supplements should be good to go with little to no work, but the other ones usually comes with more substantial changes to core mechanics. I would recommend searching for or asking about which books people would recommend for second edition. I know that Mage has a few first edition books that are still recommended.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer for <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/410713/Bloodlines-The-Ageless\" target=\"_blank\">Bloodlines: The Ageless</a> and <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/product/491569/Fire--Revolution-Carthians\" target=\"_blank\">Fire &amp; Revolution: Carthians</a> on STV<br /> Some other stuff I've done: Ordo Dracul Mysteries: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1138490-idea-coil-of-owls?p=1140522\" target=\"_blank\">Mystery of Smoke</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/1227848-mystery-of-%C5%BEiva-house-rule-revision\" target=\"_blank\">Revised Mystery of \u017diva</a> Mage The Awakening: <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/0B2LMDRtfCcpdZDU1dWJzT1dNeEE/view\" target=\"_blank\">Spell Quick Reference</a> (single page and landscape for computer screens)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11887-tessie","name":"Tessie","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11887_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4406"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-03-01T12:33:48-08:00","id":1372179,"title":"","content":"The rules did change from First Edition to Second Edition. Also, in addition, the name &quot;World of Darkness&quot; got rebranded to &quot;Chronicles of Darkness&quot; to avoid confusion with the Old or Classic World of Darkness. This happened a little <i>after</i> the Second Edition switchover, so in fact, if you're looking to separate First Edition books from Second Edition books, some &quot;New World of Darkness&quot; branded books will fall on the Second Edition part of the divide along with the Chronicles of Darkness branded books.<br /> <br /> The vast majority of narrative content is compatible across First to Second Edition. Mechanical content varies. The basic structure of the system is unchanged (nine Attributes and twenty-four Skills rated up to five dots each, construct dice pools by adding trait values, rolling 8-10 is a success, in combat order by Initiative and penalize attacks by your Defense, etc.) but certain subsystems have been altered to varying degrees. Weapon ratings now add bonus damage to successful attacks instead of bonus dice to attack rolls, so Armory, at the very least, needs to be viewed with a grain of salt. (The rough rule of thumb is to subtract one from a 1e weapon bonus to get a 2e weapon rating.) Innocents, on the other hand, should be just fine, and most of Asylum should be usable. (Derangements and Flaws have been wholly removed, but in a pinch, both can be represented as Persistent Conditions in the new Condition system.)<br /> <br /> Compatibility between monster gameline supplements varies more, as most of them introduce more fundamental mechanical changes. Vampire supplements should be mostly fine, and in fact Blood Sorcery: Sacraments and Blasphemies is explicitly the model on which Vampire 2e's blood sorcery was designed, with the intent of retroactive compatibility. Hunter supplements and maybe Mummy supplements also should be largely fine. Everything else has some kind of major mechanical overhaul that introduces difficulty. For example, Werewolf 2e removes dot ratings from most Gifts and redesigns Harmony around 5 being the ideal with problems for going to either high or low extremes, Mage 2e removes vulgar and covert spell aspects altogether and instead bases the risk of Paradoxes on a system of Reaching beyond your safe grasp, and Promethean 2e restructures Transmutations not as dot-ranked but as sets of scaling powers which you &quot;equip&quot; more of as you progress on a given Refinement.<br /> <br /> Again, narrative content is generally much more compatible than mechanical content. The Second Edition settings mostly draw and build on the First Edition settings, and Hunter is the only Second Edition that moves the &quot;timeline&quot; forward and introduces chronological changes.<br /> <br /> <b>Edit:</b> If you want to read for yourself what does and doesn't change between First and Second Edition, the <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/114078/World-of-Darkness-GodMachine-Rules-Update\" target=\"_blank\">God-Machine Rules Update</a> is offered as a free PDF.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":522,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","user":"Stupid Loserman","date":". 03-01-2020, 03:38 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/522-stupid-loserman","name":"Stupid Loserman","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar522_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1824"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}